@node Introduction
@chapter Introduction

Tag is an editor-independent plain text file indexing format that is used to establish connections between multiple files and items. This is done so by referencing one Tag item inside another in a Tag file. Tag files end with the @file{.tag} extension.

Tag is largely inspired by late German sociologist Niklas Luhmann's @i{Zettelkasten} method.

@node Structure of Tag files
@section Structure of Tag files

Tag is an indexing format. This means Tag stores the list of all the files and items of your interest along with their titles, relationships, and what to make of them. Imagine the file system your computer is your kitchen cabinet. The files on your computer are foods and ingredients disorderly placed on the cabinet. Then tag is a personal reference book where you keep track of all the items on your cabinet by giving them an unique identifier along with where they are located on the cabinet, what you can do with them, and how one is related to another. By keeping a list of all your items in such manner defines the structure of your project/directory and streamlines your workflow. Here's an example Tag file:

@example tag
@@1.8 | Platypus  | #animals #land  | :src: platypus.txt
@@1.9 | Octopus   | #animals #ocean | :src: octopus.txt
@@2.9 | Creatures | #animals | :ref: @@1.8 @@1.9 | :src: crit.txt
@@3.3 | Tentacles | #animals | :ref: @@1.8 | :src: tentacles.txt
@end example

@noindent Here, each items are separate by new lines. All the items are sorted by their unique identifier, tag number. This is important as it makes item search much easier. The components of each items are separated by the pipe (@kbd{|}) symbol. The first component of any item has to be its identifier i.e. the tag number. Tag number is the only mandatory component of any Tag item. The component followed by the tag number in the example is the title of the item. Like every other component other than the tag number, it is optional. The component followed by the title in the example is the flag list. These are used for labels and categorization. Lastly, the @code{:src:} property refers to the path to the content of the item i.e. its source and the @code{:ref:} is the list of all the other items the current item references. The components of an item shouldn't be broken down into multiple lines. And, neither should multiple items be declared in one line. Having one line per item keeps everything neatly organized.

@node How Tag Numbering works
@section How Tag Numbering works

Tag marks, sorts, and identifies items with alphanumeric floating point numbers. This makes item look-up and referencing a lot easier. For example, let's say we have four items with the tags @samp{@@1.0, @@2.0, @@3.0, @@4.0}:

@example tag
@@1.0
@@2.0
@@3.0
@@4.0
@end example

@noindent And, we want to insert a new item after @samp{@@1.0} but we don't want to change the tag number of the old items. What we do here is tag the new item a number between @samp{1.0} and @samp{2.0} and then insert the new item. @samp{1.1} will suffice here. So, the five sections are @samp{@@1.0, @@1.1, @@2.0, @@3.0, @@4.0} where the new @samp{@@1.1} is between @samp{@@1.0} and @samp{@@2.0}:

@example tag
@@1.0
@@1.1 <-- the newly inserted item
@@2.0
@@3.0
@@4.0
@end example

@noindent The tag number of an item is permanent. We need permanent identification number so we can easily reference items and not worry about it ever changing.

Note that a tag can be any positive number that isn't already taken by another item. The tag for the new item could have been any number between @samp{1.0} and @samp{2.0} such as @samp{@@1.01}, @samp{@@1.5}, @samp{@@1.9}, etc. We have infinite possibilities to choose from.

@node Valid Tag numbers
@section Valid Tag numbers

Tag numbers are positive alphanumeric floating-point numbers consisting of Latin letters and Arabic numerals that are used identifiers for tag items. Tag numbers are represented with an @samp{@@} followed by the number. For example, @samp{@@1.0}, @samp{@@1.1}, @samp{@@1.19}, @samp{@@1.B2}, @samp{@@12.5}, @samp{@@786.25}, @samp{@@300000000.59}, and @samp{@@0.0000000001} are all valid tag numbers. The radix-point character (@kbd{.}) is used to separate the whole number from the fraction.

The tag number is alphanumeric which means the tag number is made out of both the numbers @kbd{0} through @kbd{9} and the letters @kbd{A} through @kbd{Z} and @kbd{a} through @kbd{z}. After @kbd{9} comes @kbd{A}. This is base 62 numeral system. This is chosen so we can express more tags with fewer digits and create more meaningful tag numbers. All the digits in base 62 numeral system in ascending order:

@example
0123456789ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZabcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz
@end example

@noindent There a few rules to choosing a tag numbers:

@itemize @bullet{}
@item
A tag number cannot contain anything other than:

@itemize @minus{}
@item
one @kbd{@@} in the beginning

@item
alphanumeric characters

@item
radix point (@kbd{.})
@end itemize

@item
The number must be unique. No two items with the same tag number.

@item
The number must be greater than zero. @samp{@@0.0} is not a valid tag number.

@item
The number must be greater than previous item's and smaller than next item's.
@end itemize

@noindent A tag number under the hood is implemented with a string mantissa and an integer exponent. This custom data type is chosen over float/double for range and precision.

@node Tag Items
@chapter Tag Items

Tag items are the single units in Tag files. Tag items are defined in one line and may have multiple components separated by the pipe (@kbd{|}) symbol. A tag item must start with its tag number component, followed by the title component if it exists; the order of rest of the components does not matter.

@node Item Title
@section Item Title

An item in Tag can be given a title. They should be placed right after the tag number. Example of items with titles:

@example tag
@@1.0 | The First Item
@@2.0 | Second Item
@@2.5 | The Item After Second Item
@@3.0 | The Last Item?
@end example

@noindent Titles are sort of special components as they don't have property key and they aren't really flags.

@node Item Flags
@section Item Flags

Flags are words or labels used to convey the quality or features of a Tag item. They must start with the hash (@kbd{#}) symbol followed by the name of the flag. Flags are boolean variables meaning an item either has a specific flag or it does not. They are case-sensitive and multiple of them can reside inside the same component. Flags can be used to label and categorize items:

@example tag
@@b.83 | "The Raft" by Stephen King   | #books #horror
@@b.98 | "Red Rising" by Pierce Brown | #books #dystopian
@@c.21 | Brother (1997)               | #cinema #crime #drama
@@c.a5 | Seven Samurai (1954)         | #cinema #drama
@end example

@noindent Some flags have special meaning to the Tag system. For example, an item can have the @code{#ARCHIVED} flag indicates it is now archived and will be ignored by the compiler:

@example tag
@@3.75 | #ARCHIVED
@end example

@noindent Todo flags can be used to turn an item into a todo item. @code{#TODO} and @code{#DONE} are the two basic todo states but more can be used and configured. Examples of items with todo flags:

@example tag
@@td.01 | Finish Reading about Flags    | #TODO
@@td.02 | Write about Biggest Overcomes | #DONE
@@td.31 | Share Tag with others         | #TODO #IMPORTANT
@end example

@node Item Properties
@section Item Properties

Items in Tag can be given named properties. Properties are key-value pairs. The key of the property need to be enclosed inside colon (@kbd{:}) symbols and it should be followed by the value, like @samp{:@var{key}: @var{value}}. Properties can be used to denote named information regarding an item:

@example tag
@@c.35 | Scarface (1983)           | :director: Brian De Palma | :rating: 4.1
@@c.39 | 12 Angry Men (1957)       | :director: Sidney Lumet   | :rating: 4.6
@@c.42 | Memories of Murder (2003) | :director: Bong Joon Ho   | :rating: 4.4
@end example

@noindent Properties can be used to establishs connection between multiple items in a Tag document:

@example tag
@@3.24 | Life of an Octopus
@@3.9o | Living in a Garden
@@3.98 | Octupus's Garden? | :ref: @@3.24 @@3.9o
@@12.1 | C main function | :dep: @@12.9
@@12.9 | C helper functions
@end example

@noindent Multiple properties must not be declared in a single component. Use one component per property declaration.

@node Linking files to Items
@section Linking files to Items

Using appropriate properties, a file can be linked to a tag item. The @code{:src:} property is used to link the source (content) of an item. For example, a tag item that has its content stored in @file{contents.txt}:

@example tag
@@1.0 | I Have Contents! | :src: contents.txt
@end example

@file{contents.txt}:

@example txt
I am the contents of @@1.0. Please think of me
as contents inside that item.
@end example

@noindent We can also denote the output file. For example, using property @code{:obj:} we can declare the desired object path for our C code:

@example tag
@@3.0 | My C Code | :sh: cc -c -o $:obj: $:src: | :obj: code.o | :src: code.c
@end example

@noindent That value given with the @code{:sh:} (shell command) property will translate to @samp{cc -c -o code.o code.c}. Running that tag item will compile @file{code.c} into @file{code.o}. 

@node Cross-referencing Items
@section Cross-referencing Items

An item in Tag can reference other items using the reference list property. The property key for reference list is @code{:ref:}. For example:

@example tag
@@1.0 | Origin of Butterflies            | :src: bfs.origins.txt
@@2.0 | The Jurrassic Period@            | :src: jurassic.txt
@@2.2 | Butterflies in Jurrassic Period? | :ref: @@1.0 @@2.0 | :src: bfs.jrc.txt
@end example

@file{bfs.origins.txt}:

@example txt
:SOURCE: [[https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10250192/]]
Butterflies are flying insects from the Lepidoptera
superfamily. Moleculars evidences suggests that
butterflies may have originated in the Cretaceous period.
@end example

@file{jurassic.txt}:

@example txt
The Jurassic is a geological period that spanned
from approximately 201 to 145 million years
ago: end of the Triassic Period to the beginning
of the Cretaceous period.
@end example

@file{bfs.jrc.txt}:

@example txt
Butterflies likely originated from the Cretaceous
period, which was 145 to 66 million years ago.
Jurassic period was 201 and 145 million years ago.
So, unless there's new evidence, butterflies probably
didn't exist during the Jurassic period.
@end example

@noindent The contents of @samp{@@2.0} references the contents of @samp{@@1.0} and @samp{@@2.0} to make a point.

@node Document Properties
@chapter Document Properties

Sometimes we need to set properties not for a particular tag item but for the whole document. These may include document header information such as the title, author, and date or variables that we want all the items in the document to be able to access. Document properties are defined at the beginning of the tag file before the definition of any tag item.

@node Document header properties
@section Document header properties

These properties can be used as the header of the document:

@table @code
@item :TITLE:
The title of the document.

@item :AUTHOR:
The author of the document.

@item :DATE:
A date or a timestamp.

@item :EMAIL:
An email address.

@item :LANGUAGE:
Language of the document.

@item :DESCRIPTION:
A short description of the document.
@end table

@node Example document header
@section Example document header

An example document with header properties:

@example tag
:TITLE: My Journal
:AUTHOR: Mike Anderson
:DATE: <1999-02-21>
:EMAIL: anderson@@little-tall.org
:LANGUAGE: en
:DESCRIPTION: My journal entries from the Little Tall Island.

@@1.04 | Proposing Molly | :src: proposing-molly.txt
@@1.23 | Graduation      | :src: graduation.txt
@@1.45 | Our Wedding     | :src: our-wedding.txt
@@2.34 | Having a son    | :src: having-son.txt
@@3.21 | Our new venture | :src: new-business.txt
@@4.12 | The Big Storm   | :src: big-storm.txt
@end example

@node Document variables
@section Document variables

You can define a property at the beginning of a tag file and use it everywhere. The header properties are also document variables. Example of document variable and its usage:

@example tag
:TITLE: How to use document variables
:AUTHOR: Doc Dopey

:x: 5
:name: :AUTHOR:

@@1.0 | :sh: echo My name is :name:
@@1.1 | :sh: echo x = :x:
@@1.2 | :sh: echo The title of this tag is :TITLE:
@end example

@noindent Running the @code{:sh:} commands of the above example would produce:

@example
My name is Doc Dopey
x = 5
The title of this tag is How to use document variables
@end example

